  "hero.title": "How React Updates the DOM",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "You click a button and your code runs `setCount(c =&gt; c + 1)`.<br>Between calling that function and seeing a new number on screen, what does React actually do?",
  "hero.lead": "Anyone who builds interfaces with React calls a state setter almost every day. The screen changes quickly, yet rarely do we stop to ask: why does writing `&lt;Counter /&gt;` not run the function right away? Why does React maintain its own linked list of nodes instead of editing the DOM directly? Why can `useState` never live inside an `if`? And why does leaving out a list `key` make an input's typed text jump to the wrong row? This essay traces one path from end to end: <strong>every data structure and algorithm between `setCount(c =&gt; c + 1)` and the browser painting new pixels on screen.</strong>",
  "hero.note": "Every internal structure here matches <strong>React 19.3.1</strong> (`react@19.3.1`, `react-dom@19.3.1`, `scheduler@0.28.0`, released 14 October 2026). Bitmasks, field names and time-slicing thresholds come from that build; later releases may adjust them. The page loads no React bundle. Nine interactive models reimplement the underlying algorithms in a few dozen to two hundred lines, and a script cross-checks them against real `react@19.3.1` on identical inputs. For what happens after the browser paints a frame, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-pixels-reach-a-screen/#ch3\">How Pixels Reach a Screen, chapter 3</a>; for another library scheduling work against a browser frame budget, see <a href=\"../how-threejs-draws-a-scene/#ch1\">How Three.js Draws a Scene</a>. This essay was written by an AI; the closing note of each chapter states what its model leaves out.",
  "ch1.title": "Two trees, two phases",
  "toc.ch1": "Two trees, two phases",
  "ch1.law": "At the moment you call `setState`, not a single DOM node on the page moves. React splits every update in two: an interruptible first half that computes without editing the screen, and a synchronous second half that commits DOM changes all at once.",
  "ch1.p1": "Look at this minimal counter. When you click the button, `onClick` invokes `setCount(c =&gt; c + 1)`. If React tried to mutate the DOM text inside `&lt;span&gt;` immediately as it walked components, then a slow subtree of two thousand nodes would freeze typing and scrolling halfway through—you cannot pause or roll back a half-edited DOM.",
  "ch1.code.aria": "A minimal React 19 counter program",
  "ch1.code": "import { useState } from 'react';\nimport { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>;\n}\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(<Counter />);",
  "ch1.p2": "React avoids half-baked screens with <strong>two trees</strong> and <strong>two phases</strong>. The tree currently visible on screen is `current`. Whenever an update arrives, React builds a draft tree in memory called `workInProgress` (wip). The <strong>render phase</strong> runs your component functions, diffs children, and sets flags only on that draft tree, touching zero DOM nodes—so it can pause every 5 ms to yield the main thread back to the browser. Only once the entire draft tree finishes does React enter the <strong>commit phase</strong>: synchronous and uninterruptible, applying DOM mutations in one sweep and flipping the root pointer `root.current` to the new tree.",
  "dg.phases.cap": "Figure 1. The five stages triggered by setCount(c => c + 1). Only Commit mutates the DOM, and Passive Effects wait until after the browser paints.",
  "dg.phases.aria": "Diagram: setCount queues a microtask, passes through the interruptible Render phase, synchronous Commit phase, browser paint, and finally asynchronous useEffect",
  "dg.phases.s1": "1. Trigger & enqueue",
  "dg.phases.s1s": "setCount queues update & microtask",
  "dg.phases.s2": "2. Render phase (interruptible)",
  "dg.phases.s2s": "Walks wip tree & sets dirty flags",
  "dg.phases.s3": "3. Commit phase (synchronous)",
  "dg.phases.s3s": "Edits DOM, flips root, layout effect",
  "dg.phases.s4": "4. Browser layout & paint",
  "dg.phases.s4s": "Computes layout & draws pixels",
  "dg.phases.s5": "5. Passive effects",
  "dg.phases.s5s": "Runs useEffect cleanup & mount later",
  "ch1.p3": "Separating draft computation from DOM mutation unlocks everything that follows. Only because `workInProgress` lives off-screen can React pause mid-render, discard stale work, or let an urgent keystroke cut ahead of a slow list filter. The next eight chapters walk that pipeline in order: what JSX returns (chapter 2), why the tree became a linked list (chapter 3), how lists diff by key (chapter 4), where hooks live (chapter 5), how dirty bits bubble upward (chapter 6), and how commits, events and priority lanes coordinate with the browser (chapters 7–9).",
  "ch1.limit": "This chapter is only the map. How many microseconds each phase takes depends on tree depth and component work; there is no single universal timing number.",
  "ch2.title": "JSX is objects, not HTML",
  "toc.ch2": "JSX is objects, not HTML",
  "ch2.law": "Writing `&lt;Counter initial={1} /&gt;` neither runs `Counter` nor creates a single tag in the browser. JSX compiles down to a plain function call that hands back a lightweight, read-only description object.",
  "ch2.p1": "The compiler turns JSX into calls to `jsx(type, props, maybeKey)` from `react/jsx-runtime`. Each call returns a small plain object (a React Element) with 4 core fields: `$$typeof`, `type`, `key`, and `props`. In React 19.3.1, `$$typeof` is `Symbol.for('react.transitional.element')`, guarding against spoofed JSON injected from untrusted data. For native tags such as `'div'`, `type` is the string `'div'`; for `&lt;Counter /&gt;`, `type` is just a function reference pointing to `Counter`.",
  "ch2.p2": "This clears up a lasting misconception: components <strong>never own DOM nodes directly</strong>. What `Counter` returns is a nested tree of descriptions containing 0 DOM nodes, and child component functions have run 0 times. Only later, when the reconciler walks that description during the render phase, does React create matching Fiber nodes and attach real DOM instances onto host fibers via `fiber.stateNode` inside `completeWork`.",
  "dg.element.cap": "Figure 2. Left: the immutable Element returned by JSX. Right: the mutable Fiber unit paired with it during reconciliation, where host nodes attach DOM elements on stateNode.",
  "dg.element.aria": "Diagram: comparing the four fields of a React Element against a Fiber node's tag, stateNode, memoizedState, and linked-list pointers",
