  "ch2.p3": "In development builds, React even freezes element props against accidental mutation. An Element is disposable per render; the long-lived structure that persists across updates, holds hook state, and tracks DOM references is the <strong>Fiber node</strong> on the right. For a tiny `&lt;Counter /&gt;` tree, the initial JSX call creates one top element and 0 DOM nodes; after reconciliation, that tree becomes 6 Fiber nodes owning 4 real DOM nodes.",
