  "ch2.say.jsx": "Right after <code>jsx()</code> runs: React holds <em>{el}</em> lightweight descriptor, component functions ran <em>{fn}</em> times, and <em>0</em> DOM nodes exist in the browser.",
  "ch2.say.reconciled": "After reconciliation walks the tree, <em>{fib}</em> Fiber nodes stand in memory (running {fn} component functions); only host Fibers allocate {dom} real DOM nodes in <code>stateNode</code>.",
  "ch2.p4": "Switch the preset to `&lt;Card&gt;&lt;Badge /&gt;&lt;/Card&gt;` and inspect the `stateNode` column: `Card` and `Badge` (`tag = 0`, `FunctionComponent`) have `stateNode = null`. Function components have no container node in the browser DOM; only host tags like `'article'` and `'strong'` (`tag = 5`, `HostComponent`) back real elements.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model omits: the `ref` field, how React 19 keeps `key` out of `props`, `_owner` / `_debugStack` dev diagnostics, and lazy or promise elements streamed from Server Components.",
  "ch3.title": "The Fiber linked list and double buffering",
  "toc.ch3": "The Fiber linked list and double buffering",
  "ch3.law": "If rendering were ordinary JavaScript recursion, you could not pause halfway through a deep tree until every call returned from the stack. React flattened recursion into three explicit pointers and two alternating trees so it can stop every 5 ms.",
  "ch3.p1": "Before React 16, rendering walked the element tree with plain recursive calls—once started, a thousand components blocked the main thread until the stack unwound. <strong>Fiber</strong> replaces the call stack with three hand-maintained pointers on each node: `child` (first child), `sibling` (next sibling), and `return` (parent). A single loop variable `workInProgress` marches iteratively through `while (workInProgress !== null && !shouldYield())`: descend along `child` via `beginWork`, finish a leaf with `completeWork`, step across to `sibling`, or climb back up via `return`.",
  "ch3.p2": "Before advancing to each next Fiber, the scheduler (`scheduler@0.28.0`) checks whether elapsed slice time has reached <strong>5 ms</strong> (`frameInterval = 5`). When the budget runs out, the loop simply breaks, posts a macrotask over `MessageChannel`, and hands the main thread back to the browser for input and painting. Next slice, React resumes from the exact `workInProgress` pointer still sitting in memory.",
  "dg.fiber.cap": "Figure 3. Fibers wire the tree via child (cyan), sibling (gold), and return (violet dashed) pointers so traversal can pause anywhere. current and wip point to each other via alternate for double buffering.",
  "dg.fiber.aria": "Diagram: five Fiber nodes linked by child, sibling, and return pointers, plus current and workInProgress double-buffered trees linked by alternate",
  "dg.fiber.n0": "HostRoot",
  "dg.fiber.n1": "App",
