  "ch3.p3": "Pausing alone is not enough—what if higher-priority work interrupts an unfinished draft? React borrows <strong>double buffering</strong> from graphics pipelines. Every Fiber carries an `alternate` pointer linking the node on `current` (what is live on screen) to its twin on `workInProgress`. While `createWorkInProgress` reuses `current.alternate` objects without fresh allocations, `current` never moves. If a higher-priority update preempts or cancels the draft, React simply drops `workInProgress` and the live screen remains intact.",
