  "ch3.say.done": "All {total} units finished across {slice} slices with <em>{yields}</em> main-thread yields. <code>root.current</code> flips to tree B and the DOM updates atomically.",
  "ch3.say.preempt": "An urgent update cut in at unit 4! React threw away the unfinished draft while the live <code>current</code> tree (A) remained <em>completely intact</em> with zero torn DOM.",
  "ch3.p4": "At the model's default 1.6 ms per node and a 5 ms budget across 11 nodes, React splits work into 4 slices and yields 3 times. Watch the live DOM indicator: until the very last unit completes, `root.current` stays pinned to tree A.",
  "ch3.limit": "The model omits: initial mount allocation when `alternate === null`, subtree bailouts when props and lanes are clean (chapter 6), and real browser `MessageChannel` callback queuing.",
  "ch4.title": "Reconciliation and why keys matter",
  "toc.ch4": "Reconciliation and why keys matter",
  "ch4.law": "Finding the minimum edit distance between two arbitrary trees takes O(n³) time—a billion steps for a thousand nodes. React cuts that to a single-level O(n) scan in two passes, leaning on element type and developer-supplied keys to recognize identity.",
  "ch4.p1": "React trims complexity to O(n) with three heuristics: differing `type` values replace the whole subtree rather than matching deeper inside; comparisons only run sibling-to-sibling at the same parent depth; and `key` identifies which old sibling corresponds to which new element across reorders.",
  "ch4.p2": "Look at how `reconcileChildrenArray` in React 19.3.1 walks a list across <strong>two passes</strong>:",
  "ch4.r1": "<strong>Pass 1 (positional match)</strong>: Starting at `newIdx = 0`, React pairs old Fibers with new Elements slot by slot. While `key` and `type` match, it reuses the Fiber in place. The instant a `key` differs, <strong>Pass 1 breaks immediately</strong>.",
  "ch4.r2": "<strong>Pass 2 (Map lookup)</strong>: If both old Fibers and new Elements remain, React dumps remaining old Fibers into a `Map` (`existingChildren`, keyed by `oldFiber.key`, falling back to `oldFiber.index` when omitted). For each remaining new element, it looks up `existingChildren.get(key)`, clones its Fiber, and compares `oldIndex` against `lastPlacedIndex` inside `placeChild`: if `oldIndex &lt; lastPlacedIndex`, the DOM node must move right (`Placement` flag); otherwise it stays put and `lastPlacedIndex` advances to `oldIndex`.",
  "dg.reconcile.cap": "Figure 4. Prepending X before [A, B, C]. With key = item.id, Pass 1 breaks at index 0 and reuses A, B, and C without moving DOM. With array indices as keys, slots 0..2 match blindly and attach A's state to X.",
  "dg.reconcile.aria": "Diagram: comparing id keys vs array-index keys when prepending a new item X to [A, B, C]",
  "dg.reconcile.old": "Before",
  "dg.reconcile.byId": "key = item.id",
  "dg.reconcile.idRes": "Mount X; reuse A, B, C with 0 DOM moves",
  "dg.reconcile.byIdx": "key = index",
  "dg.reconcile.idxRes": "Slots 0..2 match blindly: A state leaks to X",
  "ch4.p3": "That explains why using <strong>array indices as keys</strong> (`key = index`) silently corrupts state on prepends or reorders: turning `[A, B, C, D]` into `[X, A, B, C, D]` with index keys gives both lists keys `\"0\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\"` at the first four slots. Pass 1 never breaks! Slot 0's old Fiber—still holding whatever draft text `'note-A'` the user typed into row A—gets paired with new data `X`.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Two-pass list diffing and key identity",
