  "ch4.say.moves": "Matched {reused} Fibers by <code>id</code>; by the <code>lastPlacedIndex</code> rule, <em>{moves}</em> older Fibers fell behind the cursor and need DOM moves, while every row's state stayed intact.",
  "ch4.say.mismatch": "Look closely: with index keys, React blindly reused {reused} Fibers by position, causing <em>{bug} local component states</em> from other rows to stick onto the wrong items—with zero console warnings.",
  "ch4.p4": "Check the default Prepend X case: with `key = item.id`, Pass 1 halts on index 0 (`'X'` vs `'A'`), Pass 2 recovers all 4 existing Fibers, mounts 1 new `X`, and performs <strong>0</strong> DOM moves on `A/B/C/D` (just inserting `X` at the front). Switch to index keys, and all 4 old Fibers match positionally, leaving <strong>2</strong> row states stranded on the wrong items.",
  "ch4.limit": "The model omits: replacing an entire subtree when element `type` changes (such as `&lt;div&gt;` becoming `&lt;span&gt;`), `reconcileSingleElement` for single-child parents, and raw string/number text children.",
  "ch5.title": "Hooks live on a linked list",
  "toc.ch5": "Hooks live on a linked list",
  "ch5.law": "A function component re-runs from top to bottom on every update, throwing away local variables each time. React stores no hook names at runtime—it hangs every hook sequentially on a singly-linked list attached to `fiber.memoizedState`.",
  "ch5.p1": "Inspect `mountWorkInProgressHook` in React 19.3.1: each hook is a five-field object (`memoizedState`, `baseState`, `baseQueue`, `queue`, and `next`). On first render, the first `useState` allocates node 0, the second `useState` links onto node 0's `.next`, and `useEffect` links onto node 1's `.next`. Nowhere in memory does React record `\"count\"` or `\"name\"`.",
  "ch5.p2": "On every subsequent render, `updateWorkInProgressHook` simply marches two cursors (`currentHook` and `workInProgressHook`) forward one `.next` step per call. Because identity depends strictly on call index, wrapping hook 0 in an `if` that skips it later shifts `useState(name)` into call #0—reading the integer `10` stored in slot 0—and shifts `useEffect` into slot 1, ending with `\"Rendered fewer hooks than during the previous render\"`.",
  "ch5.p3": "Where do multiple `setCount(c =&gt; c + 1)` calls in one handler queue up? Each state hook's `queue.pending` holds a <strong>circular singly-linked list</strong>: `pending` always points to the <em>last</em> enqueued update, whose `.next` wraps right back to the <em>first</em> update so enqueueing takes O(1) time. Next render, `updateReducer` breaks the ring (`first = last.next; last.next = null`) and replays updates sequentially from `baseState` (10). Any lower-priority update skipped in that pass—along with every update queued behind it—is copied into `baseQueue` so later passes preserve exact ordering.",
  "dg.hooks.cap": "Figure 5. Fiber.memoizedState chains Hooks in strict call order; Hook 0 owns a circular queue.pending ring where last.next wraps around to first.",
  "dg.hooks.aria": "Diagram: Fiber.memoizedState linked list of Hook 0, Hook 1, and Hook 2, with a circular pending update ring attached below Hook 0",
